[Music]  welcome back to cbs mornings as we  celebrate the holidays we're also  remembering those who served our nation  including service members who fought in  afghanistan iraq and syria there's now a  bipartisan push on capitol hill to build  a memorial on the national mall to honor  their sacrifice but the effort has run  into some political obstacles in  congress chris van cleave reports on the  fight to overcome those hurdles  chief warrant officer jonathan farmer  loved every minute with his family that  is silly the 37 year old special forces  soldier was killed by an isis suicide  bomber while deployed to syria in 2019 a  father of four he is one of about 7 000  u.s service members killed in the  20-year global war on terror what do you  miss the most about your husband his  hugs after a long day  his hugs  that was the most healing thing and  dancing  he loved dancing we met his wife tabitha  and his two oldest children betsy and  devin on the national mall where they  hope one day a memorial to his sacrifice  will stand it's been 20 years and  everyone keeps talking about we will  always remember we will always do this  and this is the time this is how we  remember them  the effort to build a national global  war on terrorism memorial was authorized  by congress in 2017 but it didn't  specify when or where construction would  begin it will be built with private  donations and three sites on the  national mall have been proposed  allowing the remembrance of our longest  war to share hollowed ground with  memorials to our founding fathers world  war ii korea and vietnam senator from  west virginia but that effort hit a snag  in september the president reserving the  right to object when senator joe manchin  blocked an effort to fast track a law  calling for its construction on the  national mall which is typically not  allowed i believe this president would  reopen the fight to locate other  memorials on the national mall and  create more controversy that will  ultimately delay the construction of  this world it would be a slap in the  face to all of the gold star families  and to all of those who have served  it's it's not right this has been our  nation's longest war iowa senator joni  ernst a republican and new hampshire  democrat maggie hassan are leading a  bipartisan effort to mandate the global  war on terror memorial be built on the  mall at least 54 other senators are also  on board getting agreement across the  aisle shouldn't be hard exactly when you  think about our veterans men and women  who serve  don't ask each other what political  party they're in as they go into danger  on behalf of their fellow americans they  work together for the cause of freedom  we know that it is the right thing to do  to place a global war on terror memorial  on the national mall we all know that  but again bureaucracy is getting in the  way for now the memorial is on hold the  foundation that will build it can't  start the design process without a  location and senator ernst an iraq war  combat veteran doesn't want to keep  families like the farmers waiting we  want to make sure that we're able to  honor those that have participated in  our nation's most recent war while they  are still with us  many of them are questioning does my  country understand  the sacrifice that i gave and we want to  tell them that yes we honor you what  would it mean for your family to have a  memorial on the mall it would mean  everything this is where everyone comes  to see our national memorials  now the bill actually unanimously passed  a senate committee chaired by joe  manchin the senators are now hoping to  attach it to the national defense  spending bill that the senate will take  up next week the foundation intends to  not only break ground but complete  construction and open the memorial to  the public perhaps right back there  by the end of the decade dana  chris a generation of men and women in  their service and as so many said in  that piece you don't want that slap in  the face at this point thank you

